WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.400
Sometimes an error message is evidence that the
database is doing its

00:00:03.412 --> 00:00:08.438
job correctly In this example patient MRN one zero
zero four is

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:12.962
connected to an admission record The relationship is
protected by a foreign

00:00:12.988 --> 00:00:17.825
key that uses ON DELETE RESTRICT That rule means
PostgreSQL should not

00:00:17.838 --> 00:00:21.088
allow us to delete the patient while a related
admission still depends

00:00:21.112 --> 00:00:25.825
on that patient record When you run the DELETE
statement PostgreSQL is

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:29.912
expected to reject it This is not simply bad luck or
bad

00:00:29.938 --> 00:00:35.725
SQL The database is protecting referential integrity
If the patient

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:39.575
disappeared while the admission still pointed to
that patient ID the admission

00:00:39.588 --> 00:00:43.362
would reference a row that no longer exists This
kind of rule

00:00:43.388 --> 00:00:47.138
is one reason we define keys and relationships when
designing a database

00:00:48.012 --> 00:00:53.462
Application code can make mistakes A database
constraint provides another layer of

00:00:53.488 --> 00:00:59.300
protection Run the example and read the error
message Try to identify

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:03.538
the foreign key or related table mentioned in the
message A useful

00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:07.538
habit is to interpret database errors instead of
immediately trying to bypass

00:01:07.562 --> 00:01:13.050
them Ask what rule was violated and why that rule
exists Constraint

00:01:13.075 --> 00:01:18.175
errors can reveal important information about
uniqueness required values valid ranges and

00:01:18.188 --> 00:01:19.788
relationships between tables
